---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");

const crates = [
  ["corvene", "The app: window, menus, keyboard shortcuts, x-corvene:// links."],
  ["corvene-ui", "Every view and dialog, in GPUI. Never touches git, the network or the disk."],
  ["corvene-core", "App state and the dispatcher that runs actions. Also the flags."],
  ["corvene-models", "Types the other crates share."],
  ["corvene-git", "gitoxide for reading, the git command for writing."],
  ["corvene-github", "The GitHub API and signing in."],
  ["corvene-store", "Settings and the repository list, in redb."],
  ["corvene-highlight", "Syntax highlighting: GitHub Desktop's, syntect and tree-sitter."],
  ["corvene-grammars", "The tree-sitter grammars."],
  ["corvene-packs", "Downloads optional parts like the grammars."],
  ["corvene-extensions", "Grammars from VS Code, Zed, Pulsar, Sublime Text and TextMate."],
  ["corvene-platform", "Keychains, editors, shells, paths, trash."],
  ["corvene-ffi", "The engine for the Compose Android app, exposed through UniFFI."],
  ["corvene-askpass", "Answers git's credential prompts on Android."],
];
---

<DocsLayout
  currentSlug="architecture"
  title="Architecture"
  description="How the code is laid out and how it talks to git."
>
  <p>
    The UI sends actions to <code>corvene_core::Dispatcher</code>. The dispatcher does the git, network and disk work,
    updates the state, and the views redraw from it. Views never do that work themselves.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Crate</th><th>Does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {crates.map(([name, text]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{name}</code></td>
          <td>{text}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="git">Git</h2>
  <p>
    Reads go through <a href="https://github.com/GitoxideLabs/gitoxide" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">gitoxide</a>,
    inside the app: status, history, diffs, file contents. No process to start, no output to parse. Results are cached by
    object id, so a refresh where nothing changed costs next to nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Writes (commit, checkout, merge, rebase, stash, fetch, push) run the <code>git</code> on your machine, like GitHub
    Desktop does. Your hooks, credential helpers, signing and config all apply. Every one of those calls goes through
    <code>corvene_git::process::run</code>, which sets up the environment, logs it and can cancel it. Corvene needs git
    2.38 or later.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ui">Drawing</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://gpui.rs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GPUI</a>, from the Zed editor, draws everything
    on the GPU: Metal on macOS, DirectX on Windows, Vulkan on Linux. Long lists like the diff and History only
    draw the rows on screen.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sizes, colours and both themes come straight from GitHub Desktop's source, and the
    <a href={`${base}/docs/parity`}>parity harness</a> checks the result against the real app.
  </p>

  <h2 id="state">Settings</h2>
  <p>
    Settings, the repository list and per-repository state live in a <a href="https://github.com/cberner/redb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">redb</a>
    database in your data folder. Tokens go to the system keychain: Keychain on macOS, Credential Manager on Windows,
    Secret Service on Linux.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
